{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '1,140p' VENUE.md; sed -n '1,85p' paper_strategy.md; sed -n '1,90p' scripts/build_provenance.py; cat scripts/log_event.py; sed -n '1,65p' paper.tex; ls /opt; ls /usr/local/bin | head; sed -n '1,90p' scripts/audit_paper.py\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '1,140p' VENUE.md; sed -n '1,85p' paper_strategy.md; sed -n '1,90p' scripts/build_provenance.py; cat scripts/log_event.py; sed -n '1,65p' paper.tex; ls /opt; ls /usr/local/bin | head; sed -n '1,90p' scripts/audit_paper.py\"","aggregated_output":"# ICLR 2026 Venue Brief\n\nUse this brief to tailor the manuscript for the International Conference on Learning\nRepresentations 2026 submission.\n\n## Hard Constraints\n- Target venue: ICLR 2026.\n- Submission type: conference paper.\n- Body-page budget: 8 to 9 pages, excluding references. A body of 8 pages counts only when its final page is at least 75% full; a shorter or sparser body fails validation.\n- Review model: double-blind. The submission must be fully anonymized.\n- Omit author names, affiliations, acknowledgements, and funding statements from the manuscript.\n- Do not include self-identifying links such as personal repositories, lab pages, or internal URLs.\n- Refer to your own prior work in the third person; never write phrasing that reveals authorship.\n- Include a separate `LLM Usage` section, which may appear in the appendix, describing the precise role\n  of LLMs in research ideation and writing. Significant LLM use must be disclosed; nondisclosure can\n  cause desk rejection.\n- The paper must satisfy the ICLR template and formatting rules selected by `--paper-format iclr`.\n\n## Audience\n- Machine learning researchers working on representation learning, optimization, architectures, and\n  empirical methodology.\n- Readers expect a clearly stated contribution, honest baselines, controlled comparisons, ablations\n  that isolate the mechanism, and explicit statements of scope and limitations.\n- Reviewers reward reproducibility and calibrated claims far more than headline numbers.\n\n## Framing Priorities\n- Lead with the research question and the specific claim the evidence supports.\n- State the contribution precisely in the abstract and introduction: what is new, what it improves,\n  and under which conditions.\n- Motivate the method mechanistically. Explain why it should work, not only that it does.\n- Support the central claim with controlled comparisons against meaningful baselines.\n- Use ablations to attribute the gain to the proposed component rather than to incidental changes.\n- Report variance across seeds or runs whenever the artifacts provide it.\n- Be explicit about the regime studied and where the result is not expected to hold.\n\n## Manuscript Guidance\n- Prefer a title and abstract that name the mechanism and the setting, not a generic improvement claim.\n- In the introduction, make the contribution list concrete and verifiable against the artifacts.\n- In the method section, give enough detail to reimplement: objective, architecture, and the\n  hyperparameters that matter.\n- In the results section, foreground controlled comparisons, ablations, sensitivity, and failure modes.\n- Include a limitations discussion that a reviewer would otherwise raise.\n- Figures must meet top-tier conference standards in both design (clean typography, consistent colors,\n  proper labels and legends, high resolution) and scientific content (clear message, no filler).\n- The template is single-column: size figures for the full text width and keep axis labels legible.\n\n## Anti-Goals\n- Do not deanonymize the submission in any way.\n- Do not overclaim generality beyond the settings actually evaluated.\n- Do not present a benchmark delta as a mechanistic insight without evidence connecting the two.\n- Do not omit baselines or ablations that the grounded artifacts already support.\n- Do not describe results as state of the art unless the comparison in the artifacts establishes it.\n- Do not pad the paper to fill the page budget.\n\n## Decision Rule\n- When venue framing and grounded evidence conflict, follow the grounded evidence.\n- Use this brief to choose emphasis, terminology, and narrative framing, not to invent stronger claims\n  than the run supports.\n# Paper strategy: cap-dependent task budgets\n\n**Status:** ready for the `paper_standalone` family. This is a framing plan, not a manuscript or a statement that final submission requirements have already been met.\n\n**Provisional title:** Cap-Dependent Task Budgets for Leaderboard Promotion.\n\n## Central question and thesis\n\n**Question.** On fixed public multitask score tables, how does the smallest task subset needed to make a target co-first change with the aggregation rule and the allowed fraction of score headroom?\n\n**Thesis.** In the evaluated score tables, changing the response cap reverses mean-versus-median investment orderings for some targets and makes other promotions unattainable. Aggregation-rule resistance must therefore be reported conditional on the cap and target score vector, rather than as a universal ranking.\n\nPresent HELM MMLU and the six-task Open LLM table as two settings of one static intervention model. Do not organize them as chronological experiments. The contribution is a conditional, reproducible score-space analysis with explicit allocation certificates and failure cases, not a training method, an architecture, or a demonstrated optimizer improvement.\n\n## Framing decision and alternatives\n\nThis is not an edit pass. No seeded prior manuscript, resolved edit-context files, `aggregated/`, or `extra_tasks/` were found. The JSON enum `prior_paper_story_status: rejected` means no inherited manuscript story is adopted; it does not mean an unseen manuscript was assessed.\n\nThe selected source and original comparator were inspected directly. The selected staged `program.py` matches the downstream source file by SHA-256. Selection metadata identifies the first candidate, not either later refinement. Recorded directory-tree hashes differ across artifact scopes; do not equate them based on a matching source file.\n\nThree candidate stories were considered:\n\n- **Prospective cap frontiers:** preserve the earlier conceptual direction, but narrow its language. Only two positive caps were evaluated; continuous frontiers, empirical response envelopes, strict-margin sensitivity, and composition resampling are not completed evidence.\n- **Broad cross-setting solver improvements:** reject as the center. There are no grounded ablations attributing gains to the later gain-window or mixture-cut variants, and search scores tie the original comparator.\n- **Measured cap-dependent promotion budgets:** select. The same-target rule comparisons directly demonstrate reversals in both score tables, while reachability and independent bounds make the limitations inspectable.\n\nThis choice beats a campaign diary because it answers the original scientific question with controlled, interpretable comparisons. Neither a positive search winner nor model training is needed for this bounded study.\n\n## Independent final assessment and evidence hierarchy\n\nRead final assessment independently of selection. The following are **private audit facts**, not names for visible scientific metrics:\n\n- `study.selection_kind = non_winner_candidate`; `study.investigation_outcome = completed_no_winner`.\n- `study.search_score = study.baseline_score = 0.7239766081871346`; `study.search_score_delta = 0`.\n- `study.attempts.total/scored/unscored = 3/3/0`. Unscored attempts would be missing performance evidence, not measured losses.\n- `final.qualification.status/outcome/correct = completed/qualified/true`.\n- `final.metric.final_score = 0.6633040935672518` belongs to a separate weighted evaluation scope. It is not a matched change from the search score.\n\nNo failed final qualification is reported in this run. Do not invent one, reinterpret non-selection as a validity failure, or treat passing assessment as proof of generalization or scientific novelty. Missing criterion diagnostics remain unknown. Candidate diagnostics are marked unverified; use recorded independent validation and reference bounds instead of self-report.\n\nMain quantitative grounding is `final.evidence.episodes`, whose payload is `paper_data.json.final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes`. Filter `solution.cases` to `case = original`, then pair by family, target, rule, fraction, and seed. Retain exact episode/case/observation pointers for downstream claim mapping. The method files and `task/resources/final/` files support implementation and provenance explanations; their paths are not invented grounding IDs.\n\n## Scientific definitions and scope\n\nFor normalized target scores `x` and a selected task subset `S`, selecting coordinate `j` changes it to `round(x_j + rho(1-x_j), 10)`. Other target coordinates and every rival score coordinate remain fixed. Investment is the uniform task count `|S|`, optionally divided by task count `d` for cross-setting display. The empirical positive caps are `rho = 1` and `rho = 0.5`; zero belongs to the no-update control.\n\nPromotion means **co-first** within aggregate tolerance `1e-10`, not strict first place. The ordinary even median averages its two central values. Borda uses average pairwise win credit with half credit for ties: changing the target can change rivals' Borda totals despite fixed rival task scores. Recompute those totals.\n\nThis is a hypothetical score-response model. It does not measure training examples, tokens, money, compute, contamination efficacy, transfer, forgetting, or rivals' strategic responses. Only under a genuine upper-response envelope could an exact support minimum lower-bound affected tasks for a successful intervention; no evidence here calibrates such an envelope to real training.\n\nThe frozen final context contains 57 HELM subjects and six normalized Open LLM columns, with 42 and 2129 rows respectively. These are resource-context facts, not the broader public-catalog totals from the task brief. Do not mix Raw columns into the six-task analysis or reinterpret their aggregate columns as all underlying subtasks. Target row numbers are local identifiers, not model names.\n\n## Narrow method groups\n\n1. **Static capped-response model, supporting:** the shared intervention and the three aggregation rules define the comparison.\n2. **Frozen allocation solver, studied/proposed:** sorted gains for mean, threshold counting for odd median, endpoint counting for even median, and bounded rival-aware integer Borda search with verified fallback. The label proposed does not assert novelty or superiority.\n3. **Original frozen comparator, baseline:** preserve its tied search comparison using `study.baseline_score` and `study.search_score_delta`. No separate matched final evaluation of that full implementation is grounded.\n4. **Evaluator-recorded policies, baselines:** no update, random task order, and immediate-gain greedy. These have matched final allocation records; do not silently relabel the evaluator's greedy policy as the original frozen comparator.\n5. **Independent budget reference, supporting:** exact flags and lower/upper bounds certify individual reported costs. Finite selected allocations match exact reference cardinalities in the original records. This is not a proof that the deadline-bounded Borda algorithm is exact on every input.\n\nExclude later gain-window and mixture-cut branches from the method narrative. They are not ablations, and their names are not evidence of an improved mechanism. Do not introduce unmeasured architecture shifts or training procedures.\n\n## Results that carry the argument\n\n### Cap-conditioned budget ordering\n\nPut this small table in the main body; each entry is a task count from `final.evidence.episodes`, not a training cost:\n\n| Score table | Local target row | Headroom fraction | Mean | Median | Borda |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| HELM | 10 | 1 | 9 | 12 | 34 |\n| HELM | 10 | 0.5 | 23 | 12 | 39 |\n| Open LLM | 1609 | 1 | 2 | 3 | 5 |\n| Open LLM | 1609 | 0.5 | 4 | 3 | 5 |\n\nThe within-target mean/median ordering reverses in each setting. These are counterexamples to an unconditional ordering, not estimates of reversal frequency in future model populations. Explain them using cumulative score gains versus order-statistic thresholds, not an untested causal account of developer behavior.\n\nShow all sampled targets in the main comparison, not only these examples. Targets were selected as nonwinners under the original **mean**, not under every rule. HELM row 40 already needs zero median updates; retain that zero without attributing it to an intervention.\n\n### Reachability, not fictitious finite budgets\n\nAt full headroom, all eight sampled targets per family are reachable under each rule. At half headroom, mean/median/Borda reachability counts are **5/5/4 for HELM** and **7/6/3 for Open LLM**, derived from the original final records. Keep the denominator at eight targets per family, not seeds times targets or controls plus originals.\n\nReport unreachable promotion as a status or infinity. Empty task lists accompanying unattainability are not zero-cost successes. Reference sentinels `d+1` are not investment values. Do not average away unattainable cases or compare finite-only averages without stating the changed population.\n\nimport hashlib\nimport importlib.metadata\nimport json\nimport platform\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nfrom build_results import ROOT, sidecar\n\n\ndef main():\n    paths = [\"paper_data.json\", \"references.bib\", \"references_provenance.json\",\n             \"data/helm.json\", \"data/openllm.json\", \"data/protocol.json\",\n             \"iclr2026_conference.sty\", \"iclr2026_conference.bst\", \"official_template.tex\"]\n    paths += [str(path.relative_to(ROOT)) for path in sorted((ROOT / \"source_evidence\").rglob(\"*\")) if path.is_file()]\n    manifest = {path: hashlib.sha256((ROOT / path).read_bytes()).hexdigest() for path in paths}\n    output = ROOT / \"derived/checksums.json\"\n    output.write_text(json.dumps({\"algorithm\": \"sha256\", \"files\": manifest,\n                                  \"scope_note\": \"Local file bytes; not interchangeable with source upstream hashes or tree hashes.\"}, indent=2))\n    sidecar(output, [\"@evidence[[data/helm.json]]\", \"@evidence[[data/openllm.json]]\",\n                     \"@evidence[[paper_data.json]]\", \"@evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation.py]]\"], kind=\"derived\")\n    source = json.loads((ROOT / \"paper_data.json\").read_text())\n    assert manifest[\"source_evidence/evaluation.py\"] == source[\"final_evaluation\"][\"evaluator_sha256\"].removeprefix(\"sha256:\")\n    environment = {\"python\": platform.python_version(), \"platform\": platform.platform(),\n                   \"paper_packages\": {name: importlib.metadata.version(name) for name in\n                                      [\"numpy\", \"matplotlib\", \"requests\", \"jsonschema\", \"pyarrow\", \"pymupdf\"]},\n                   \"evaluated_dependencies_file\": \"requirements-evaluated.txt\",\n                   \"note\": \"Paper extraction/plotting environment is distinct from the recorded evaluation environment. No evaluation rerun.\"}\n    (ROOT / \"derived/environment.json\").write_text(json.dumps(environment, indent=2))\n    provenance = {\n        \"venue\": \"ICLR 2026 anonymous submission\", \"repository\": \"https://github.com/ICLR/Master-Template\",\n        \"download_url\": \"https://github.com/ICLR/Master-Template/archive/refs/heads/master.zip\",\n        \"retrieved_utc\": \"2026-11-07\", \"archive_subdirectory\": \"Master-Template-master/iclr2026\",\n        \"files\": {path: manifest[path] for path in [\"iclr2026_conference.sty\", \"iclr2026_conference.bst\", \"official_template.tex\"]},\n        \"modifications\": \"None to official style, bibliography style, or retained reference template. Manuscript omits identifying authors and does not enable final-copy mode.\",\n        \"geometry\": {\"text_width_inches\": 5.5, \"text_height_inches\": 9.0},\n        \"checklist\": \"No separate checklist is included in the official ICLR 2026 template. Supplement includes reproducibility, limitations and LLM usage disclosures; no checklist from another venue is substituted.\",\n    }\n    (ROOT / \"template_provenance.json\").write_text(json.dumps(provenance, indent=2))\n    upstream = {}\n    for family in [\"helm\", \"openllm\"]:\n        resource = json.loads((ROOT / \"data\" / (family + \".json\")).read_text())\n        upstream[family] = {\"acquired_utc\": resource[\"acquired_utc\"], \"scale\": resource[\"scale\"],\n                            \"tasks\": resource[\"tasks\"], \"license_note\": resource[\"license\"],\n                            \"sources\": sorted({(record[\"source_url\"], record[\"source_sha256\"]) for row in resource[\"rows\"] for record in row[\"provenance\"]})}\n    path = ROOT / \"derived/upstream_provenance.json\"\n    path.write_text(json.dumps(upstream, indent=2))\n    sidecar(path, [\"@evidence[[data/helm.json]]\", \"@evidence[[data/openllm.json]]\"], kind=\"derived\")\n    for name, kind, inputs in [\n        (\"paper.tex\", \"manuscript\", [\"paper_data.json\", \"references_provenance.json\", \"source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py\"]),\n        (\"claims_grounding.json\", \"derived\", [\"paper_data.json\", \"references_provenance.json\", \"derived/original_records.json\"]),\n        (\"figure_manifest.json\", \"derived\", [\"paper_strategy.json\", \"derived/original_records.json\"]),\n        (\"paper.pdf\", \"manuscript\", [\"paper.tex\", \"references.bib\", \"figures/score_space_overview.png\", \"figures/paired_mean_median_budgets.pdf\", \"figures/cap_conditioned_reachability.pdf\"]),\n    ]:\n        path = ROOT / name\n        if not path.exists():\n            continue\n        token = \"@\" + \"evidence[[\"\n        payload = {\"schema_name\": \"scientific-evidence.artifact-manifest\",\n                   \"artifact\": {\"path\": name, \"kind\": kind, \"sha256\": hashlib.sha256(path.read_bytes()).hexdigest()},\n                   \"derived_from\": [token + item + \"]]\" for item in inputs]}\n        if name in [\"paper.pdf\", \"figure_manifest.json\"]:\n            payload[\"artifact\"].pop(\"sha256\")\n            payload[\"notes\"] = [\"Parent orchestration renders the overview and recompiles the PDF after this stage. Mutable output has an input trail but no pre-render content digest.\"]\n        Path(str(path) + \".prov.json\").write_text(json.dumps(payload, indent=2))\n    print(\"Wrote file-scope checksums, upstream provenance, template provenance, environment and artifact sidecars.\")\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\nimport datetime\nimport json\nimport sys\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\n\ndef log_event(step, summary, evidence, next_action):\n    event = {\n        \"timestamp\": datetime.datetime.now(datetime.timezone.utc).isoformat(),\n        \"scope\": \"paper\",\n        \"step\": step,\n        \"summary\": summary,\n        \"evidence\": evidence,\n        \"next_action\": next_action,\n    }\n    text = \"\\n### {timestamp} — {step}\\n{summary}\\nEvidence: {evidence}\\nNext: {next_action}\\n\".format(**event)\n    for path in [Path(\"memory.md\"), Path(\"global_memory/memory.md\").resolve()]:\n        with path.open(\"a\") as stream:\n            stream.write(text)\n    with Path(\"global_memory/memory.jsonl\").resolve().open(\"a\") as stream:\n        stream.write(json.dumps(event) + \"\\n\")\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    log_event(*sys.argv[1:])\n\\documentclass{article}\n% --- watermark (auto-inserted) ---\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{xfp}% \\fpeval; in the kernel since 2022, harmless on older LaTeX\n\\usepackage{draftwatermark}\n\\newlength{\\watermarkTextWidth}\n\\AddToHook{begindocument/end}{%\n  \\SetWatermarkText{\\normalfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\SetWatermarkFontSize{10pt}%\n  \\SetWatermarkLightness{0.85}%\n  \\settowidth{\\watermarkTextWidth}{\\normalfont\\fontsize{10pt}{12pt}\\selectfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkScale{\\fpeval{0.8 * sqrt(\\paperwidth**2 + \\paperheight**2) / \\watermarkTextWidth}}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkAngle{\\fpeval{atand(\\paperheight/\\paperwidth)}}%\n  \\SetWatermarkScale{\\watermarkScale}%\n  \\SetWatermarkAngle{\\watermarkAngle}%\n}\n% --- end watermark ---\n\n\\usepackage{iclr2026_conference,times}\n\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,booktabs,graphicx,longtable,subcaption}\n\\usepackage{hyperref,url}\n\\title{Cap-Dependent Task Budgets for Leaderboard Promotion}\n\\newcommand{\\budget}{B}\n\\newcommand{\\tol}{\\varepsilon}\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nMultitask leaderboards aggregate heterogeneous scores, creating incentives to improve selected benchmark tasks rather than broad capability. How much targeted investment is needed to reach the top, and does the answer depend on the aggregation rule? We study a static score-space model: investment selects tasks, each selected score receives a bounded fraction of its remaining headroom, and competitors remain fixed. We compare arithmetic mean, ordinary median, and pairwise Borda aggregation on public HELM MMLU and Open LLM score snapshots. Independently checked allocation bounds reveal cap-dependent reversals: for one HELM target, mean versus median budgets change from 9 versus 12 tasks at full headroom to 23 versus 12 at half headroom; an Open LLM target exhibits the same qualitative reversal. Some promotions become unattainable under the smaller cap. These results reject a cap-independent resistance ordering on the evaluated instances. They quantify conditional score-space vulnerability, not the training data, compute, or money needed to realize such changes.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes/0/solution/cases/0/observations]]\n% @evidence[[derived/original_records.json]]\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Introduction}\nPublic multitask benchmarks compress a model's performance profile into a leaderboard position. MMLU and BIG-Bench Hard exemplify evaluations with heterogeneous component tasks, while HELM emphasizes transparency and breadth of evaluation \\citep{Hendrycks2020MMLU,Suzgun2022BBH,Liang2022HELM}. Once adoption and recognition depend on position, a developer may have an incentive to concentrate resources on a subset of benchmark tasks. A leaderboard's aggregation rule determines which score changes are rewarded. Evaluating that incentive requires specifying both the allowed changes and the meaning of investment.\n\nWe ask a deliberately conditional question: on a fixed public score table, what is the smallest task subset whose improvement would make a target model co-first? Investment is the number of selected tasks, not the amount of training data. Each selected coordinate receives a stipulated fraction of its remaining score headroom; every unselected coordinate and every competitor's coordinate stays fixed. This model separates a leaderboard's response to score changes from the unresolved question of how reliably training can produce those changes. It is an analysis of a possible response envelope, not evidence that a developer can realize the envelope.\n\nA common intuition is that the median should resist concentrated improvements because isolated extreme scores need not affect its value. That intuition does not settle the relative cost of reaching the top. A mean rewards every increment, whereas a median depends on crossing order-statistic thresholds. Reducing the permitted increment can change which threshold crossings remain available without proportionally changing the median's task budget. Conversely, a low starting median can require many changes even when a mean responds cheaply. The relevant object is therefore a budget conditioned jointly on the target, aggregation rule, and response cap.\n\nOur study makes three concrete contributions. It defines a common bounded intervention model for mean, median, and Borda; expresses scalar allocation through sorting or threshold certificates and Borda through rival-aware integer constraints; and reports paired budgets, unattainability, and matched comparator results on frozen public score tables. The central finding is not a universally preferable aggregation rule. The same-target mean--median ordering reverses when the cap changes in both evaluated settings. Reference bounds and explicit failure states make that conclusion auditable without interpreting a timeout as impossibility.\n\nThe study is intentionally narrower than a strategic training equilibrium. Competitors do not react, task costs are uniform, and no model is trained. These restrictions preserve an interpretable comparison but preclude claims about real resource expenditure, general capability, or a population-wide frequency of reversal. The full target panel, including targets already co-first under a different rule, is retained rather than filtering to favorable examples.\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/score_space_overview.png}\n\\caption{The score-space intervention model. A fixed public score table and target define the comparison. Selecting tasks raises only the target's selected coordinates to a specified headroom cap. Mean, ordinary median, and rival-aware Borda yield a minimum task budget or certified unattainability. The cap describes a hypothetical response, not a measured training effect.}\n\\label{fig:overview}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\paragraph{Evaluation design and incentives.}\nThe benchmark lottery shows that task selection can alter comparative conclusions \\citep{Dehghani2021Lottery}. Here the task set stays fixed while the aggregation rule and permitted response change. HELM's multidimensional evaluation and the language-model evaluation harness motivate explicit task definitions and reproducible score processing \\citep{Liang2022HELM,Gao2023Harness}; our computations use released scores rather than rerunning either evaluation system. Analyses of leaderboard incentives and selective evaluation provide complementary reasons not to equate rank with general capability \\citep{Singh2025Illusion}. Strategic post-training has also been modeled as an interaction between a benchmark designer and competing developers \\citep{Chen2026Strategic}. We instead hold rivals fixed and measure discrete promotion budgets, without estimating an equilibrium or latent quality.\n\n\\paragraph{Social choice and targeted changes.}\nTreating tasks as voters connects model evaluation with social choice \\citep{Lanctot2023SocialChoice,Brandt2016Handbook}; aggregating rankings is also a classical information-retrieval problem \\citep{Dwork2001Rank}. Strategic preference reporting \\citep{Gibbard1973Manipulation} and election bribery \\citep{Faliszewski2009Bribery} establish useful distinctions between aggregation and manipulation. Our intervention changes only a target's numerical task coordinates, according to a fixed response cap. It cannot arbitrarily rewrite rankings or remove competitors. In particular, a Borda calculation must update rivals as the target overtakes them, rather than compare against frozen Borda totals.\n\n\\paragraph{Robustness and contamination.}\nClassical robust estimation distinguishes local influence from resistance under a specified contamination model \\citep{Huber1964Robust,Hampel1974Influence,Rousseeuw1984Median}. These distinctions caution against importing a slogan about median robustness into an adaptive, bounded, top-rank objective. Our coordinates are heterogeneous tasks, not independent observations of a common location parameter. Benchmark leakage and rephrased contamination can affect measured model performance \\citep{Zhou2023Cheater,Yang2023Rephrased}, but those findings do not calibrate our headroom response. We neither identify contaminated models nor infer that task-specific data necessarily produces the score increments analyzed here. The contribution is an instance-level comparison under stated assumptions, not a claim of first priority over all work on strategic evaluation.\n\n\\section{Method}\n\\subsection{Problem setup and intervention model}\nautoillustrate-venv\ndrawio\nheadless\n2to3\n2to3-3.12\nautoillustrate\nclaude\ncodex\ndocutils\ndotenv\ndrawio\ngemini\nheadless\nimport json\nimport re\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nimport jsonschema\nimport pymupdf\n\n\nROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent\n\n\ndef main():\n    schema = json.loads((ROOT / \".codex/skills/validate-paper/templates/figure_manifest.schema.json\").read_text())\n    manifest = json.loads((ROOT / \"figure_manifest.json\").read_text())\n    jsonschema.validate(manifest, schema)\n    strategy = json.loads((ROOT / \"paper_strategy.json\").read_text())\n    specs = {item[\"figure_id\"]: item for item in strategy[\"figure_specs\"]}\n    for figure in manifest[\"figures\"]:\n        spec = specs[figure[\"figure_id\"]]\n        assert figure[\"n_panels\"] <= spec[\"max_panels\"]\n        assert max(figure[\"categories_per_panel\"]) <= spec[\"max_categories_per_panel\"]\n        assert max(figure[\"series_per_panel\"]) <= spec[\"max_series_per_panel\"]\n        assert figure[\"panels_per_row\"] <= 3\n        assert figure[\"estimated_min_rendered_font_pt\"] >= 8.5\n        if figure[\"generator\"] == \"matplotlib\":\n            assert (ROOT / figure[\"path_pdf\"]).is_file()\n            assert (ROOT / figure[\"path_png\"]).is_file()\n    text = (ROOT / \"paper.tex\").read_text()\n    resolved_pointers = 0\n    for target, locator in re.findall(r\"@evidence\\[\\[([^#\\]]+)(?:#([^\\]]+))?\\]\\]\", text):\n        path = ROOT / target\n        assert path.is_file(), target\n        if locator.startswith(\"/\") and path.suffix == \".json\":\n            value = json.loads(path.read_text())\n            for component in locator[1:].split(\"/\"):\n                component = component.replace(\"~1\", \"/\").replace(\"~0\", \"~\")\n                value = value[int(component)] if isinstance(value, list) else value[component]\n            resolved_pointers += 1\n    for family in [\"helm\", \"openllm\"]:\n        table = (ROOT / \"tables\" / (family + \"_complete.tex\")).read_text().strip()\n        assert table in text, family + \" supplementary table differs from generated source\"\n    assert len(re.findall(r\"@\\w+\\{\", (ROOT / \"references.bib\").read_text())) in range(25, 31)\n    document = pymupdf.open(ROOT / \"paper.pdf\")\n    reference_index = next(index for index, page in enumerate(document) if re.search(r\"(?m)^REFERENCES\\s*$\", page.get_text()))\n    assert 8 <= reference_index <= 9, reference_index\n    last_page = document[reference_index - 1]\n    boxes = [block[:4] for block in last_page.get_text(\"blocks\")\n             if block[0] >= 104 and block[2] <= 512 and block[1] >= 70 and block[3] <= 725]\n    boxes += [tuple(info[\"bbox\"]) for info in last_page.get_image_info()]\n    maximum_y = max(box[3] for box in boxes)\n    span_fraction = (maximum_y - 72) / (9 * 72)\n    if reference_index == 8:\n        assert span_fraction >= .75, span_fraction\n    body_text = \"\\n\".join(page.get_text() for page in list(document)[:reference_index])\n    banned = re.compile(r\"\\b(?:idea|ideas|program|programs|attempt|attempts|eligible|worker_01|worker_02|combined_score|rubric)\\b\", re.I)\n    assert not banned.search(body_text), banned.search(body_text).group(0) if banned.search(body_text) else \"\"\n    result = {\"figure_schema_pass\": True, \"figure_density_pass\": True,\n              \"manuscript_json_pointers_resolved\": resolved_pointers,\n              \"embedded_supplementary_tables_match\": True, \"body_pages\": reference_index,\n              \"last_body_content_y_end_pt\": maximum_y,\n              \"last_body_span_fraction_of_9in_from_y72\": span_fraction,\n              \"geometry_note\": \"Local text/graphics bounding-box sanity check, not a substitute for the parent orchestrator's authoritative rendered page validator.\",\n              \"anonymous_template\": True, \"banned_body_language_absent\": True,\n              \"concept_figure_render_pending\": not (ROOT / \".concept_figure_complete\").exists()}\n    (ROOT / \"derived/local_audit.json\").write_text(json.dumps(result, indent=2))\n    print(json.dumps(result, indent=2))\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
